---
description: Update AIMetrics block in file headers for all modified or created files
alwaysApply: true
---

<!-- core:start -->
Every file you create or modify must have an AIMetrics block in its file header comment.

**Exempt files** carry no AIMetrics block, because they carry no header at all
(see the `licence-header` rule for the full list): dotfiles such as `.gitignore`;
lockfiles and machine-generated files; and the `agents/rules/*.mdc` files
themselves.

- If a file already has an AIMetrics block, update it by adding a new entry to the array (do not remove existing entries).
- If a file has no AIMetrics block, add one.
- For new files created entirely by the agent, set contribution value to 100.
- For modified files, estimate the percentage of lines changed and use that as the contribution value.

Each entry records one agent/model contribution to the file. Log one entry per
**change**, not one per edit: a change made through several edits in sequence is
a single contribution.

- `timestamp` — for a change made in one pass: the date and time of the
  modification, ISO 8601 format (YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss).
- `interval` — for a change made through several edits in sequence: an ISO 8601
  interval covering them, from the first edit to the last (e.g.
  `2026-02-14T15:30:00/2026-02-14T15:52:00`). Use this instead of `timestamp`
  rather than adding an entry per edit, which turns a record of who contributed
  what into a log of keystrokes. Commit messages always use `interval`, because
  a commit summarises a session's work.
- `name` — the name of the agent or tool that made the modification. This must identify the
  agent running the current session (e.g. the harness package name, such as `@deepseek-ai/dsh`).
  Do NOT copy the values from the examples below; they are illustrative only.
- `version` — the version of that agent/tool, taken from the current session (e.g. `0.2.0-rc.2`).
- `model` — the identifier of the model powering the agent in the current session
  (e.g. `deepseek-flash`).
- `contribution.value` — the percentage of the file's lines changed by this contribution
  (100 for a new file).

The `name`, `version`, and `model` fields must always describe the agent and model that
actually performed the modification.

In every format below, substitute `interval` for `timestamp` when the change
spanned several edits in sequence.

The format for JavaScript and Node files (`.mjs`, `.js`, `.cjs`), in `//` comments, is:

```
// AIMetrics: [
//     {
//     "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
//     "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
//     "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
//     "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
//     "contribution": {
//       "value": <percentage>,
//       "units": "%"
//       }
//     }
//   ]
```

The format for Java files (inside the `/* */` header comment) is:

```
 * AIMetrics: [
 *     {
 *     "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
 *     "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
 *     "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
 *     "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
 *     "contribution": {
 *       "value": <percentage>,
 *       "units": "%"
 *       }
 *     }
 *   ]
```

The format for Python, YAML, shell, and other `#`-commented files is:

```
# AIMetrics: [
#     {
#     "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
#     "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
#     "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
#     "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
#     "contribution": {
#       "value": <percentage>,
#       "units": "%"
#       }
#     }
#   ]
```

The format for Markdown files (inside `<!-- -->` comments) is:

```
  AIMetrics: [
      {
      "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
      "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
      "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
      "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
      "contribution": {
        "value": <percentage>,
        "units": "%"
        }
      }
    ]
```

The format for XML files, inside the `<!--+ ... +-->` block, is:

```
    | AIMetrics: [
    |     {
    |     "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the modification, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
    |     "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
    |     "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
    |     "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
    |     "contribution": {
    |       "value": <percentage>,
    |       "units": "%"
    |       }
    |     }
    |   ]
```

Place the block after the `</meta:header>` tag and before the closing comment delimiter.

For files with no AIMetrics or AI contribution, the block should be:

```
 * AIMetrics: []
```

(using the appropriate comment prefix for the file type).

### Git commit messages

Every git commit message created by an agent must end with an AIMetrics block.

- The `name`, `version`, and `model` fields are the same as the values used for a file header
  (see above): they must always describe the agent and model that actually performed the work.
- The `contribution.value` is an estimate of how much of the changes in the commit were
  contributed by the agent. If all of the changes were contributed by the agent, set the value
  to `100`.
- A commit message block uses `interval` instead of `timestamp`: an ISO 8601 interval covering
  the current session (e.g. `2026-02-14T15:30:00/2026-03-14T05:00:00`).

The format for an AIMetrics block at the end of a commit message is:

```
AIMetrics: [
    {
    "interval": "<ISO 8601 interval covering the current session, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00/2026-03-14T05:00:00>",
    "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
    "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
    "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
    "contribution": {
      "value": <percentage>,
      "units": "%"
      }
    }
  ]
```

### GitHub issues, pull requests and comments

Any content an agent creates or posts on GitHub must end with an AIMetrics
block, for the same reason a commit message does: it is an authored artifact,
and the block records which agent and model produced it. That covers issues,
pull requests, issue and pull request comments, reviews, releases, and any
other GitHub content that carries a Markdown body.

- The `name`, `version`, and `model` fields are the same as the values used for
  a file header and a commit message: they must always describe the agent and
  model that actually performed the work.
- Use `timestamp` for a change made in one pass, or `interval` when the content
  was written or revised through several edits in sequence — one entry covering
  them, exactly as for a file header. (A commit message always uses `interval`,
  because a commit summarises a session's work.) Append a new entry for a later
  change rather than replacing existing ones.
- The `contribution.value` is `100` for content the agent wrote in full, and an
  estimate of the lines changed for content it later edited.
- Surround the block with a fenced code block, because a GitHub body is Markdown
  and has no comment syntax to put it in. It must be the last thing in the body.
- A pull request body is a separate artifact from the commit messages carried by
  the pull request. Each commit message carries its own block, and the pull
  request body carries another.

The format for an AIMetrics block at the end of a GitHub issue, pull request,
comment, review or release body is:

````text
```text
AIMetrics: [
    {
    "timestamp": "<ISO 8601 timestamp of the issue, e.g. 2026-02-14T15:30:00>",
    "name": "<agent/tool name from the current session>",
    "version": "<agent/tool version from the current session>",
    "model": "<model identifier from the current session>",
    "contribution": {
      "value": <percentage>,
      "units": "%"
      }
    }
  ]
```
````

### Cross-project issue and pull request references

A reference to an issue or pull request is only unqualified (`#123`) in the
project that owns it. Anywhere else — in an issue, a comment, a release or a
pull request in a different project — write it as `owner/repo#123`.

An unqualified number in the wrong project does not simply fail to link. If that
project has an issue with the same number, the reference silently points at the
wrong one.

The closing keywords follow the same rule, because GitHub resolves them exactly
as it resolves a plain reference:

- `Closes owner/repo#123`
- `Fixes owner/repo#123`
- `Resolves owner/repo#123`

Two related facts:

- `Refs` is **not** a closing keyword. A body or commit full of `Refs #123`
  closes nothing.
- A closing keyword can sit in a pull request body or in a commit message, but a
  commit message only takes effect once it reaches the default branch. On a
  feature branch, the pull request body is the reliable place.

